The root set
============

What the garbage collector begins its mark phase from, and which bank of
virtual memory each thing reachable from it lives in.

Two banks
---------

An execution context has two memory files: local and global. Which one an
allocation lands in is chosen by the allocation mode, which `setglobal`
alters and `currentglobal` reports.

PLRM 3.7.2 gives the rule that shapes everything below: a global object may
not reference a local one. Local may reference global freely. So a local
dictionary holding a global array is ordinary, and the reverse raises
`invalidaccess`. Six dictionaries `systemdict` holds are the sanctioned
exception, and the collector marks them as such.

PLRM 3.7.3 exempts global virtual memory from `save` and `restore`: what a
program puts there survives a restore that ends the save it was made in.
PLRM 3.7.4 states the consequence for reclamation -- garbage collection is
the only way storage in global virtual memory is ever recovered, since save
and restore do not reach it.

Both banks are collected. A collection marks them together in one pass,
because an object in one may be named from the other and a walk that
stopped at the boundary would take a named object for garbage, and then
reclaims whichever banks it was asked for. A bank is reclaimed only by a
collection that marked it, which is what PLRM 8.2's vmreclaim chooses
between: it turns automatic collection off for one bank or for both, and
performs an immediate collection of one or of both.

Everything below is therefore a root in earnest -- an object reachable
only through one of them, and not marked, is taken by the next collection
that runs.

In the tree below, (G) is global and (L) is local.

